WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.480
A neuron can receive an inhibitory input that moves its membrane potential by zero

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.060
millivolts. It can still stop the cell from firing.

3
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.320
How? The usual picture says inputs are voltages that add and subtract.

4
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.240
That picture cannot explain this one.

5
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:26.173
Think of each input as a conductance instead.

6
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:32.233
When a synapse opens channels, it adds conductance, measured in nanosiemens.

7
00:00:33.053 --> 00:00:37.433
Those channels have a reversal potential, the voltage at which current through them

8
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:43.753
is zero. Every open conductance pulls the membrane toward its own reversal potential.

9
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:48.407
Here is a made-up cell, with the same voltage everywhere.

10
00:00:49.007 --> 00:00:50.527
All values are illustrative.

11
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:58.407
At rest, the leak conductance is 10 nS, reversing at −65 mV.

12
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:00.647
Threshold is −50 mV.

13
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:07.127
Now open an excitatory conductance of 5 nS, reversing at zero.

14
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.867
The voltage settles at −43.3 mV.

15
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:16.827
The voltage is an average of the reversal potentials, weighted by conductance.

16
00:01:17.487 --> 00:01:19.067
That is above threshold.

17
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.060
Now add an inhibitory conductance of 10 nS.

18
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.900
Its reversal potential equals rest.

19
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.320
The average now includes a second pull toward rest.

20
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.060
The voltage settles at −52 mV.

21
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.420
That is below threshold, so there is no spike.

22
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.740
Take the excitation away, and the inhibition alone leaves the voltage at −65 mV.

23
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.880
The trace does not move, yet it vetoed the spike.

24
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:52.613
Inhibition like this is called shunting.

25
00:01:53.093 --> 00:01:58.313
It lowers the input resistance, so the same excitatory current makes a smaller

26
00:01:58.313 --> 00:02:05.673
voltage change. It also shortens the time constant, from 20 ms to 10 ms. Give

27
00:02:05.673 --> 00:02:11.013
the cell two brief pulses, 10 ms apart, each worth 2 mV.

28
00:02:11.993 --> 00:02:15.653
Without the shunt, they add up to 3.21 mV.

29
00:02:15.653 --> 00:02:18.853
With the shunt, only 2.74 mV.

30
00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:22.133
The shunt did not shrink each jump.

31
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:25.993
It left less of the first pulse for the second to ride on.

32
00:02:26.817 --> 00:02:33.197
So an inhibitory input is defined by what it does to firing, not by which way the

33
00:02:33.197 --> 00:02:36.957
trace moves. Inputs add as conductances.

34
00:02:37.797 --> 00:02:41.177
Each one pulls the voltage toward its own reversal potential.

35
00:02:42.157 --> 00:02:45.817
A shunt can leave the voltage alone and still veto the spike.
